NAME
    sort - sort lines of text

SYNOPSIS
    sort [FILE...]
    sort -r [FILE...]
    sort -n [FILE...]
    sort -k FIELD                       (JSONL mode)

DESCRIPTION
    Reads input lines from each FILE (or stdin if none), sorts them,
    and writes the sorted output to stdout. Default sort is
    lexicographic (byte-by-byte ASCII).

    -r    reverse the sort order
    -n    numeric sort (compare leading digit runs as integers)
    -k FIELD  JSONL mode: parse input as JSONL records, sort by the
              value of FIELD. Numeric vs string is auto-detected
              per-record. Used inside `|>` pipelines.

    Sort is in-memory and bounded by the input buffer; very large
    streams (>~256 KiB) may be truncated.

EXAMPLES
    sort names.txt
    sort -r names.txt                   # reverse
    ps |> sort -k pid                   # sort processes by pid
    ls /etc | sort                      # ls already does this

EXIT STATUS
    0   sort completed
    1   I/O error or buffer overrun

SEE ALSO
    uniq(1), grep(1), pluck(1)
